  "text": "Let's determine the largest prime divisor of the expression \\(2^{33} + 3^{29}\\).\n\n### Step 1: Factor the expression\nWe start by factoring the expression:\n\\[\n2^{\\color{red}33} + 3^{\\color{red}29} = 2^{\\color{red}{7}} \\cdot 2^{\\color{orange}{26}} + 3^{\\color{orange}{26}} \\cdot 3 = 2^{\\color{orange}{26}} \\cdot (2^{\\color{orange}{7}} + 3) = 2^{\\color{orange}{26}} \\cdot (64 + 3) = 2^{\\color{orange}{26}} \\cdot 67\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Identify the prime divisors\nFrom the expression:\n\\[\n2^{\\color{orange}{26}} \\cdot 67\n\\]\n- The prime numbers involved are 2 and 67.\n\n### Step 3: Determine the largest prime divisor\nThe largest prime divisor in the expression is \\(67\\).\n\n### Final answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{67}\n\\]",
